::PC_U05_L03_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U05_L03_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.4
Question:
What is the modulus of 3 + 4i?
{
~A. 4#This uses only the imaginary part. Modulus uses both parts.
=B. 5#Correct. sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5.
~C. 7#This adds the parts instead of using the distance formula.
~D. sqrt(7)#This does not square and add both parts correctly.
}
::PC_U05_L03_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U05_L03_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.4
Question:
Which point represents 5(cos 60 degrees + i sin 60 degrees)?
{
~A. (5, 60)#This lists polar values, not rectangular coordinates.
~B. (60, 5)#This reverses the polar values.
~C. (5cos 5, 5sin 5)#This uses the wrong angle.
=D. (5cos 60 degrees, 5sin 60 degrees)#Correct. Rectangular coordinates are (r cos theta, r sin theta).
}
::PC_U05_L03_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U05_L03_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.5
Question:
When multiplying complex numbers in trigonometric form, what happens to the moduli?
{
=A. They are multiplied.#Correct. Products in trigonometric form multiply moduli and add arguments.
~B. They are added.#Arguments are added, not moduli.
~C. They are subtracted.#Subtraction is not the multiplication rule.
~D. They are ignored.#The modulus is required because it controls distance from the origin.
}
::PC_U05_L03_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U05_L03_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.5
Question:
Multiply 2(cos 20 degrees + i sin 20 degrees) and 4(cos 30 degrees + i sin 30 degrees).
{
~A. 6(cos 50 degrees + i sin 50 degrees)#This adds moduli instead of multiplying them.
~B. 8(cos 10 degrees + i sin 10 degrees)#This subtracts arguments instead of adding them.
=C. 8(cos 50 degrees + i sin 50 degrees)#Correct. Multiply moduli: 2 times 4 = 8. Add arguments: 20 degrees + 30 degrees = 50 degrees.
~D. 2(cos 60 degrees + i sin 60 degrees)#This uses the wrong modulus and angle.
}
::PC_U05_L03_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U05_L03_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.4; MLA.PC.VC.5
Question:
Why is trigonometric form useful for multiplying complex numbers?
{
~A. It avoids all calculations.#Calculations are still required.
=B. It separates distance and direction, making multiplication rules clearer.#Correct. Trigonometric form lets you multiply moduli and add arguments.
~C. It changes every complex number into a real number.#A complex number usually remains complex.
~D. It removes the imaginary part.#The imaginary part is still represented through sine.
}